  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 319 AM CDT Mon Aug 10 2026\n\nSeasonably fast zonal flow continues across the northern tier of the \nCONUS this Monday morning, with broad ridging in place over the \nsouthern half of the country. Along the northern periphery of the \nridge, water vapor imagery shows a broad plume of upper-level \nmoisture pointed across northern Kansas. Scattered high-based \nshowers and weak thunderstorms are again ongoing within this \nmoisture plume, mostly towards the Nebraska border. Expecting less \nprecipitation coverage by the daytime hours, but there should still \nbe thick mid and high clouds across much of the area throughout the \nafternoon. If a few breaks in the clouds can occur, temperatures \ncould still quickly warm up towards the 100 degree mark, especially \nacross central Kansas. But at this point think the pervasive cloud \ncover will keep temperatures in the low/mid 90s, especially north of \nI-70. Still hot nonetheless, with heat indices near/above 100, but \nwill hold off on any Heat Advisory as a result.\n\nThe pattern remains similar Tuesday and Wednesday. We should see a \ncontinuation of the upper-level moisture plume, resulting in more \nperiods of thick high clouds. This will keep temperatures a touch \ncooler than they otherwise could be. However with the low-level \nthermal ridge axis remaining nearby (700 mb temps ~15C), heat will \nstay in place. Still expecting temperatures in the mid/upper 90s \nwith heat indices around or slightly above 100. Given persistent \nsoutherly winds keeping the boundary layer somewhat mixed, overnight \nlows will also remain warm - near 80.\n\nFor the second half of the week, temperatures are trending hotter. \nMost guidance is keeping the trough over the Great Lakes less \namplified, resulting in the cold front not being able to make its \nway as far south over eastern KS. Most likely scenario now is for it \nto stall somewhere near the forecast area Wednesday night, staying \nstationary or only slowly sliding south through Saturday. This \nincreases the potential for the early week heat to continue another \nextra day or two, especially south of I-70. Wherever it does end up, \nwe'll see shower and thunderstorm chances continue along and north \nof the boundary.",
